namespace Ponca
{
/*!
  \page ponca_concepts Concepts reference

  \tableofcontents

  %Ponca does not rely on virtual interfaces. Its classes are templates assembled at compile time, and the types
  provided by users (points, weight kernels, neighbor filters, computational objects) are only required to define a
  given set of types and functions. These sets of requirements are called *concepts*. This page lists them, and is the
  reference for the template parameters documented as "Implements ...Concept" in the C++ API.

  Two kinds of concepts coexist in the library:
  - **documentation concepts**, described on this page with a reference declaration taken from the file
    `doc/examples/concepts.hpp` (namespace `Ponca::Concept`). This file is only read by Doxygen: it is not compiled
    and there is no base class to inherit from;
  - **C++20 concepts**, written with `requires` clauses, that check at compile time that the classes are combined
    correctly (e.g. Ponca::IsPoint, Ponca::ProvidesAlgebraicSphere). They are listed in \ref concepts_cpp20, and the
    capabilities of the fitting classes are detailed in \ref fittingreference_outputandcapabilities.

  \note The rationale for this design is given in \ref ponca_points. Most computations in %Ponca are very small
  (accumulating a few sums per neighbor), so the cost of virtual calls would be significant, while templates let the
  compiler inline and optimize the combination of all the computations.

  \section concepts_point Point concept

  A point type describes how %Ponca accesses the data of one sample. The minimal declaration is:

  \snippet concepts.hpp PointConcept

  | Member                                    | Required by                                                                 |
  |-------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
  | `enum { Dim }`                            | all classes (dimension of the ambient space, e.g. 3)                        |
  | `Scalar`, `VectorType`                    | all classes (Ponca::ProvidesCommonTypes); `VectorType` must have the API of `Eigen::Matrix<Scalar, Dim, 1>` |
  | `MatrixType`                              | neighbor filters (Ponca::DistWeightFilter, Ponca::NoWeightFilter, ...) and covariance-based methods; define it as `Eigen::Matrix<Scalar, Dim, Dim>` |
  | default constructor                       | containers, kd-trees, point generation functions                            |
  | `pos() const`                             | all classes (Ponca::IsPoint)                                                |
  | `pos()` (write access)                    | only the point generation functions of the Common module                    |
  | `normal() const`                          | methods using normals (Ponca::ProvidesNormal): Ponca::MeanNormal, Ponca::OrientedSphereFit, Ponca::UnorientedSphereFit, Ponca::MeanPlaneFit, Ponca::CNC |

  The accessors may return a reference to an `Eigen::Matrix`, or any object implicitly convertible to it, such as an
  `Eigen::Map` over an external buffer (this is how Ponca::PointPositionNormalBinding avoids copies).

  To be usable in CUDA kernels, every member function must be declared with the `PONCA_MULTIARCH` macro, see
  \ref ponca_gpu_page.

  Ready-to-use point types are listed in \ref points_utility.

  \section concepts_weighting Weight kernel concept

  A weight kernel is a 1D function \f$\phi\f$ mapping a normalized distance \f$x = \|\mathbf{q}-\mathbf{p}\|/t\f$
  to a weight. It is used by Ponca::DistWeightFilter, see \ref fitting_neighborhoods.

  \snippet concepts.hpp WeightKernelConcept

  In addition to `f`, `df` and `ddf` (the kernel and its first and second derivatives), a kernel declares three
  compile-time flags:

  | Flag                             | Meaning                                                                                    |
  |----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
  | `static constexpr bool isCompact` | `true` if \f$\phi(x) = 0\f$ for \f$x \geq 1\f$: Ponca::DistWeightFilter then gives a null weight to every point farther than \f$t\f$ without evaluating the kernel |
  | `static constexpr bool isDValid`  | `true` if `df` is valid; required to compute derivatives (Ponca::KernelProvidesFirstOrderDerivative) |
  | `static constexpr bool isDDValid` | `true` if `ddf` is valid; required by second order derivatives (Ponca::KernelProvidesSecondOrderDerivative) |

  The kernels provided by %Ponca are listed in \ref fitting_kernel.

  \section concepts_neighborfilter Neighbor filter concept

  A neighbor filter is constructed for each evaluation, from an evaluation position \f$\mathbf{p}\f$ and a scale
  \f$t\f$. It tells, for any point, its weight and its coordinates expressed in a local frame. The library provides
  Ponca::DistWeightFilter, Ponca::NoWeightFilter, Ponca::NoWeightFilterGlobal and Ponca::FilterWithAttributes (see
  \ref fitting_filters). The general API is:

  \snippet concepts.hpp FilterConcept

  More precisely, a neighbor filter must:
  - define the types `DataPoint`, `Scalar`, `VectorType`, `MatrixType`, `NeighborhoodFrame` (the frame class it
    derives from) and `WeightReturnType` (a pair `{weight, local position}`),
  - be constructible from `(const VectorType& center, Scalar scale)` (this constructor is used by the
    `setNeighborFilter({p, t})` idiom), and by default,
  - provide `operator()(const DataPoint& q)`, returning the weight of `q` and its coordinates in the local frame;
    points with a null weight are ignored by Ponca::Basket::addNeighbor,
  - provide `frame()` (the neighborhood frame, see below) and `evalScale()` (the scale \f$t\f$, used for instance by
    the scale-invariant GLS parameters),
  - provide the derivatives of the weight with respect to the scale and to the evaluation position (`scaledw`,
    `scaled2w`, `spacedw`, `spaced2w`, `scaleSpaced2w`). They are only called when derivatives are requested (see
    \ref fitting_derivatives), and can return 0 for filters that do not depend on \f$\mathbf{p}\f$ or \f$t\f$.

  These requirements are checked by Ponca::IsNeighborhoodFilter and Ponca::IsNeighborhoodFrame.

  \subsection concepts_frame Neighborhood frames

  Every filter derives from a *neighborhood frame*, which converts coordinates between the global frame of the point
  cloud and the local frame used internally by the computations:

  | Frame                          | Local coordinates                                           | `hasLocalFrame` |
  |--------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------|
  | Ponca::CenteredNeighborhoodFrame | \f$\mathbf{x}' = \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{p}\f$ (positions only, directions are unchanged) | `true` |
  | Ponca::GlobalNeighborhoodFrame   | \f$\mathbf{x}' = \mathbf{x}\f$                              | `false`         |

  A frame provides `center()`, `changeNeighborhoodFrame(newCenter)`, `convertToLocalBasis(q, isPositionVector)` and
  `convertToGlobalBasis(q, isPositionVector)`, where `isPositionVector` must be `false` for directions (normals,
  gradients, displacements), which are not affected by translations. Some primitives require a centered frame to
  remain accurate (e.g. Ponca::AlgebraicSphere requires Ponca::HasLocalFrame). See \ref fitting_frames for practical
  consequences.

  \section concepts_computObjectBasket Computational object concept (Basket extensions)

  The fitting module combines *computational objects* in a Ponca::Basket. Each of them is a class template
  parameterized by the point type, the neighbor filter and its base class `T`, from which it derives (this is the
  Curiously Recurring Template Pattern explained in \ref crtpestimator):

  \snippet concepts.hpp ComputationalObjectConcept

  - `PONCA_FITTING_DECLARE_DEFAULT_TYPES` defines `Base` (alias for `T`), `Scalar`, `VectorType` and
    `NeighborFilter`. Note that this macro ends with a `public:` section.
  - `init()` resets the internal state, `addLocalNeighbor(w, localQ, attributes)` accumulates one neighbor of weight
    `w`, whose position in the local frame is `localQ` and whose attributes (position, normal, ...) are given by
    `attributes`, and `finalize()` computes the result and returns a Ponca::FIT_RESULT. Each of them must first call
    the version of its base class (`Base::init()`, `Base::addLocalNeighbor(...)`, `Base::finalize()`).
  - In the current API, `addLocalNeighbor` returns `void` (the snippet above shows the former `bool` return type).
  - The requirements on the other members of the basket (e.g. "an algebraic sphere must be provided") are expressed
    with a `requires` clause on C++20 capability concepts (`ProvidesREQUIREMENT_1<T>` above).

  Writing new computational objects is detailed in \ref fitting_extending_page.

  \section concepts_computObjectBasketDiff Computational derivatives concept (BasketDiff extensions)

  Ponca::BasketDiff extends an existing basket to compute derivatives with respect to the scale and/or the evaluation
  position. Its extensions follow the same principle, with an additional template parameter giving the
  differentiation type (a combination of Ponca::FitScaleDer and Ponca::FitSpaceDer):

  \snippet concepts.hpp ComputationalDerivativesConcept

  - `PONCA_FITTING_DECLARE_DEFAULT_DER_TYPES` defines `ScalarArray` (a row vector with one entry per derivative) and
    `VectorArray` (a `Dim` x number-of-derivatives matrix).
  - `addLocalNeighbor` receives the derivatives `dw` of the weight of the neighbor, laid out as described in
    \ref fitting_derivatives_layout.

  \section concepts_computeobject Compute objects

  A *compute object* is any class deriving from Ponca::ComputeObject. It provides the functions that iterate over a
  neighborhood and return a Ponca::FIT_RESULT:
  - `compute(begin, end)`: process the points of an iterator range,
  - `computeWithIds(ids, points)`: process the points `points[i]` for every index `i` of the range `ids` (e.g. the
    result of a kd-tree query),
  - `compute(container)`: shortcut for `compute(std::begin(container), std::end(container))`, only available on the
    CPU.

  Ponca::Basket, Ponca::BasketDiff and Ponca::CNC are compute objects, and so are the objects returned by the
  Ponca::Factory. The evaluation schemes (Ponca::SingleEvaluationScheme, Ponca::MLSEvaluationScheme) take a compute
  object as first argument.

  \section concepts_cpp20 C++20 concepts used by the library

  | Domain                | Concepts                                                                                          |
  |-----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
  | Points                | Ponca::ProvidesCommonTypes, Ponca::IsPoint, Ponca::ProvidesNormal, Ponca::Is3D                    |
  | Filters and kernels   | Ponca::IsNeighborhoodFrame, Ponca::IsNeighborhoodFilter, Ponca::ProvidesNeighborhoodFilter, Ponca::HasLocalFrame, Ponca::KernelProvidesFirstOrderDerivative, Ponca::KernelProvidesSecondOrderDerivative |
  | Baskets               | Ponca::ProvidesComputeState, Ponca::ProvidesBasketUnitBase, Ponca::ProvidesBasketDiffUnitBase, Ponca::ProvidesScaleDerivative, Ponca::ProvidesSpaceDerivative, Ponca::ProvidesDerivatives |
  | Fitting capabilities  | `Ponca::Provides*` concepts, see \ref fittingreference_outputandcapabilities                      |
  | Containers            | Ponca::ValidCapacity                                                                              |

  When a requirement is not met, the compiler reports which concept is not satisfied, see
  \ref fitting_newcapabilities for an example.
*/
} // namespace Ponca
